## Decisions

**"readme" as the new category name, not "scaffold-readme" or "readmeBadge".** The category enum values across the codebase are single nouns (`methodology`, `commands`, `agents`, `skills`, `mcp`, `brain`, `ide`). Following that pattern, `"readme"` is the natural successor to `"badge"`. The category now covers the entire README concern (creation + badge), not just badge injection, so a name that describes the artifact (`readme`) is more accurate than one that describes a single action (`badge`).

**Absorb injectBadge entirely rather than wrapping it.** The spec explicitly says scaffoldReadme "absorbs the responsibilities of the former injectBadge category." The insertion logic (`insertBadge` function) is the only reusable piece — it moves to `scaffoldReadme/insertBadge/` as a helper. The orchestrator logic (README existence check, status mapping) is rewritten because the control flow changes: the old module returned `would-skip` when no README existed, while the new module creates one. Wrapping the old module would mean carrying dead code paths.

**Delete injectBadge/ rather than deprecating it.** The old module has no external consumers — it was imported only by `src/tools/init/index.ts` and `src/tools/upgrade/index.ts`. Both are rewired in this plan. Leaving the old folder would create dead code and confuse agents navigating the tree. The spec says "retires it as a separate category," and the progressive disclosure docs say dead code costs tokens and confuses agents.

**No `would-skip` status in scaffoldReadme.** The old `injectBadge` returned `would-skip` when no README existed because it refused to create one. The new module always has a path forward (create or inject), so the only terminal status is `exists`. This simplifies the upgrade mapping: `would-create` maps to `"missing"`, `exists` maps to `"matches"`.

**insertBadge helper is a verbatim lift from injectBadge, not a rewrite.** The two-phase scan logic (frontmatter skip + badge strip count) is battle-tested with comprehensive tests. Moving it to its own helper subfolder maintains the progressive disclosure pattern without touching proven logic.

**BADGE_LINE and BADGE_DETECTION exported as named exports from insertBadge/.** The orchestrator needs BADGE_DETECTION for the existence check and BADGE_LINE for the README creation path. Co-locating these constants with the insertion logic keeps them next to their primary consumer while making them available to the orchestrator. The spec says "the badge line is a constant inlined in the helper, not read through initContent."

**Step ordering: readme stays last in both init and upgrade.** The badge category was already last. README scaffolding has no dependents — no other category reads README.md — so keeping it last maintains the existing wizard flow and avoids reordering risk.
